HARD RULES
1. Tokens only. Every color, spacing value, radius and font size comes from the CSS
   variables in globals.css and the Tailwind theme. Never write a raw hex, rgb(),
   Tailwind default color class (bg-blue-500, text-gray-600), or arbitrary pixel value
   (p-[13px], mt-7) in a component.
2. Spacing is always a multiple of 4px, taken from the scale.
3. All validation lives in lib/validation/auth.ts as shared zod schemas. One email rule,
   one password policy, reused everywhere. No per-screen validation, no duplicated regex.
   Never display a rule in the UI that you do not also enforce before submit.
4. Light and dark ship together. Dark mode uses the dark token set only — never a
   light-mode hex dropped into dark.
5. WCAG AA always: real <label> elements, visible focus rings, errors announced via
   role="alert", aria-busy on loading buttons, 4.5:1 text contrast, and never convey
   state by color alone. prefers-reduced-motion disables all animation.
6. Frontend only. All API calls are mocked in lib/api/auth.ts as typed async functions
   that resolve after ~800ms. No backend, no database, no auth provider.
7. Reuse before you create. Check components/auth/ for an existing component before
   building a new one. Do not create component variants that were not asked for.
8. Never expose raw backend error text to the user. Never reveal whether an email
   address is registered.

CORAL RULE — the most important rule in this project
Coral (#FF6B4A light / #FF8266 dark) is reserved EXCLUSIVELY for AI-powered features.
The authentication module contains no AI feature. Coral must therefore NOT appear in any
button, link, icon, badge, focus ring, spinner, skeleton, gradient, chart or
illustration in this module. Every accent is indigo (#5B4FE9 light / #7C71F0 dark).
The single permitted exception is the small "AI Interview Platform" pill in the left
brand panel. If you are ever unsure, use indigo.

LOADING STATES
Do not add skeletons or loading.tsx files unless I explicitly ask for them. Most pages in
this module render instantly and a skeleton would only add a flash of grey.

Every button that fires an async call uses the shared loading prop, which disables it so
double-submit is impossible. That is the only loading state needed by default.

If I do ask for a skeleton: mirror the real layout exactly so nothing shifts, flat blocks
in the border token (#E5E7EB light / #2E2E42 dark), 8px radius for input-shaped blocks,
subtle pulse, no shimmer sweep, no spinner, no indigo, no coral. Compose it from one
shared <Skeleton> primitive. aria-hidden on the wrapper. Never skeletonize the left brand
panel.

WORKFLOW
Build only what the current message asks for. Do not scaffold future screens. Do not
refactor or restyle files unrelated to the current request. If a change would touch a
file I did not mention, say so and ask first. End every response with a one-line list of
files created or changed.